    "pipeline": "Step 1: DataRetrieverAgent -&gt; Retrieve a US county boundary dataset. -&gt; Step 2: VectorAnalysisAgent -&gt; Filter the county boundary dataset to only counties in the contiguous United States, excluding Alaska, Hawaii, Puerto Rico, and other outlying territories, while preserving the county identifier needed for a later join to 2021 population data. -&gt; Step 3: DataRetrieverAgent -&gt; Retrieve a tabular dataset of 2021 resident population by U.S. county/county-equivalent for all states and DC, suitable for joining to the existing contiguous U.S. county boundaries. Use an official source such as the U.S. Census Bureau, and ensure the table includes a stable county identifier that matches the preserved county identifiers in the boundary dataset plus one numeric 2021 population field. Do not use any dataset that is supposed to contain county geometries; a non-spatial table is preferred. -&gt; Step 4: VectorAnalysisAgent -&gt; Join the 2021 county population table to the contiguous U.S. county boundary layer by constructing the standard 5-digit county identifier from the state and county codes in the table and matching it to the preserved county identifier in the boundaries. Keep only records for the contiguous United States, verify the join coverage is complete or note any unmatched counties, and ensure the joined population attribute is stored as numeric for later quantile classification. -&gt; Step 5: MapProjectionAgent -&gt; Reproject the joined county dataset to a Lambert Conformal Conic coordinate reference system. -&gt; Step 6: MappingAgent -&gt; Create the county-level choropleth map from the reprojected contiguous U.S. county dataset by symbolizing 2021 population with 5 quantile classes, using the joined population attribute and preserving the Lambert Conformal Conic projection.",
